# OpenPete - Spyro PC port (@VERSION@, @PLATFORM@)

OpenPete plays the three PlayStation Spyro games natively on your PC: Spyro
the Dragon (1998), Spyro 2: Ripto's Rage! (1999) and Spyro: Year of the
Dragon (2000). You supply the discs; OpenPete ships no game content.

## Documentation

The documentation ships with this build: open `docs/index.html` in a
browser. It covers requirements, controls, settings, file locations,
controllers, mods and troubleshooting. It is also at
https://openpete.com/docs/user/.
<!-- @linux -->
In the AppImage it sits inside the read-only image: run
`./OpenPete-*.AppImage --appimage-extract` once and open
`squashfs-root/usr/docs/index.html`.
<!-- @end -->

## Quick start

1. Dump your disc to a `.bin`/`.cue` pair: Spyro the Dragon (NTSC-U,
   SCUS-94228), Spyro 2: Ripto's Rage! (NTSC-U, SCUS-94425) or Spyro: Year
   of the Dragon (NTSC-U, SCUS-94467).
<!-- @windows -->
2. Run `openpete.exe`. Windows shows a SmartScreen warning ("Windows
   protected your PC") for an unsigned download: click "More info", then
   "Run anyway".
<!-- @end -->
<!-- @linux -->
2. Run `chmod +x OpenPete-*.AppImage` then `./OpenPete-*.AppImage` for the
   AppImage, or `./openpete` from the extracted `.tar.xz`.
<!-- @end -->
3. The game page shows one box per game. Drag your `.bin` or `.cue` onto
   the window, or pick it in a file dialog: choose an empty box (Enter, or
   the South face button, Xbox A), or press Space or the West face button
   (Xbox X). Adding a disc copies the image into OpenPete's library and
   compiles that game for your machine once, then returns to the game page.
4. Choose a box with a disc (Enter, the South face button or a click) to
   start its game. A later start opens the game page again, unless the
   command line names a game or a disc.

## Mods

A mod with a `src/` folder runs code inside the game process with the same
access to your files and network as the game itself. There is no sandbox.
Install only mods you trust, as you would any other program. A mod without a
`src/` folder never runs code.

